---
/**
 * Advertising and independence.
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS BEFORE THERE IS A SINGLE ADVERTISER
 *
 * Written now, with nothing sold and nobody to please, because that is the only
 * moment it can be written honestly. A policy drafted after the first sponsor
 * asks for something is a negotiation, not a policy.
 *
 * It is also the most useful sales document this site will have. A local
 * business that reads it knows exactly what it is buying and exactly what it
 * cannot buy, and that is a shorter conversation than the alternative.
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Advertising & Independence — Delaware Field Guide"
  description="What a sponsor can and cannot buy on Delaware Field Guide. Advertising never changes a listing, a ranking, a verification status or a caveat."
>
  <div class="wrap pt-11 pb-6">
    <p class="label m-0">Policy</p>
    <h1 class="text-[2.2rem] sm:text-[2.9rem] mt-3.5 mb-0 max-w-[16ch] text-marsh">
      Advertising does not change the Field Guide
    </h1>

    <p class="mt-5 mb-0 text-[1.15rem] leading-[1.5] max-w-[54ch]">
      Nothing on this site is sponsored yet. This is written now, while there is
      nobody to disappoint, because that is the only time it can be written
      honestly.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose-field pb-4">
    <h2>What a sponsor can buy</h2>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      A clearly labeled position beside content their customers are already
      reading. That is the whole product: proximity to a decision, at the moment
      somebody is making it.
    </p>

    <h2>What a sponsor cannot buy</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Being added to a listing they don't otherwise belong in</li>
      <li>Moving higher in any result, list or map</li>
      <li>A stronger verification label than the facts support</li>
      <li>Removal of an accurate caveat, warning or blank</li>
      <li>Changing a rule, fee, hour or policy as published here</li>
      <li>Being called the best of anything</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      If a business tells me something on its own listing is wrong, I check it
      and fix it — whether or not they have ever paid me a penny. That is not a
      courtesy extended to advertisers. It is the entire point of the site.
    </p>

    <h2>Where sponsorship will never appear</h2>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Not beside emergency veterinary information, blue-green algae warnings,
      nesting closures, permit rules or anything else somebody is reading in
      order not to get hurt or overcharged. Not between a question and its
      answer.
    </p>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      This one is enforced in the code rather than in my judgement: those routes
      are on a blocklist, and the build fails if a commercial module reaches one.
      I would rather it be impossible than remembered.
    </p>

    <h2>Things the Field Guide sells itself</h2>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Stickers, and eventually printed and downloadable guides, marked
      <strong>From the Field Guide</strong>. Those are mine, so there is no
      independence question — but there is a different one, and the answer is
      the same: <strong>a product never decides what gets covered.</strong> A
      place earns a page by being worth writing about. If a page exists only to
      hang a product on, it should not exist.
    </p>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      The useful information stays free. Products package it or make it portable.
      They never withhold it.
    </p>

    <h2>Affiliate links</h2>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      There are none yet. If there are, they will be disclosed where they appear,
      and nothing will be recommended because it pays better. Gear gets
      recommended because it solves the trip.
    </p>

    <h2>Who builds this</h2>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      The site is designed and built by <a href="https://werth.design" rel="noopener">Werth Design</a>,
      which is my studio. That is a maker's credit, not advertising, and it buys
      nothing here either — if a Werth Design client turns up in a listing, they
      are held to the same standard as everybody else, and being a client is
      never a reason to be covered.
    </p>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/about/">Who writes this</a></li>
      <li><a href="/verified/">The verification index</a> — every fact, its source and its age</li>
      <li><a href="/changed/">What changed in Delaware</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Layout>
